B.ce(L, (71, 15, 21),
     'Monday, 8 p.m., Paris\n'
     'Dear Diary,\n'
     'What a day! This morning I was so disappointed when I met Ausable. I had always imagined a secret agent to be tall, slim and handsome, dressed in a dark coat, meeting mysterious dark-eyed beauties and escaping from enemies with a pistol in his hand. '
     'Instead, Ausable was fat – very fat – with an American accent, living in a small, gloomy room on the sixth floor of a shabby hotel. There was nothing romantic about him.\n'
     'But tonight I saw what a real spy is. When we entered his room, Max was waiting with a pistol to steal the secret report about new missiles. I was terrified, but Ausable was perfectly calm. '
     'He sat down heavily and grumbled about the “balcony” through which people kept entering his room. Then he said the police were coming. When there was a knock at the door, Max, believing every word, climbed out of the window to hide on the balcony – and fell, because there was no balcony at all! The knock was only Henry, the waiter, bringing a drink.\n'
     'I am full of admiration. Ausable did not need muscles or a gun – his presence of mind and quick imagination defeated an armed enemy. I have learnt that we should never judge a person by his looks.\nFowler', lid='sr3')
B.ce(L, (71, 22, 27),
     'Max’s Thoughts\n'
     '“At last! The fat American has come. He is not dangerous – I have the pistol. The report on the missiles will soon be mine,” I thought.\n'
     'But then he started grumbling about a balcony. A balcony? I had come in with a passkey. “It might have saved me some trouble had I known,” I said. Still, something began to worry me.\n'
     'Then Ausable said he had asked the police for extra protection and that they would come at any minute. My heart started beating fast. If the police found me with a gun, they would shoot me without hesitation. '
     'When the knocking came at the door, I panicked. I bit my lip and my face burned with anger. “I will go out on the balcony,” I thought. “If anyone enters, I will shoot.”\n'
     'I moved quickly to the window, threw my legs over the sill and jumped down, expecting to land on the balcony… There was nothing under my feet! In that last moment, as I fell and screamed, I understood – there was no balcony. The fat man had tricked me with words alone.', lid='sr3')
B.ce(L, (71, 28, 32),
     'The Lady in Red\n'
     'I had planned to steal the jewels from Shotover Grange, but I could not open the safe myself. When I saw Horace Danby, the lock-maker, at work, I knew luck was on my side.\n'
     'I walked in calmly in my red dress, as if I owned the house. I patted Sherry, the dog, and straightened the ornaments on the fireplace to show that I was at home there. '
     'I smiled at Horace and said I didn’t expect to meet a burglar – I acted amused, not afraid, so that he would feel hopeful.\n'
     'When he begged me not to call the police, I pretended to be kind. I said I had forgotten the combination of the safe and needed my jewels for a party, and that I would let him go if he opened it. '
     'He was so nervous and so polite that he didn’t suspect anything. He even took off his gloves to work – leaving his fingerprints everywhere – while I never removed mine.\n'
     'When the safe opened, I took the jewels, thanked him sweetly and drove away. I never touched anything. The police found only his fingerprints. I used his good manners, his fear of the police and his trust to walk away with the fortune.', lid='sr4')
B.ce(L, (71, 33, 38),
     'Statement to the Police\n'
     'To\nThe Inspector of Police,\nShotover Police Station.\n\n'
     'Sir,\nI, Mrs ………, wife of the owner of Shotover Grange, wish to report a robbery at our house.\n'
     'My family and I were in London for some weeks. Only our two servants remained at the Grange. On the afternoon of the robbery they had gone to the movies, so the house was empty.\n'
     'When I returned, I found the safe behind the painting in the drawing room open and all my jewels missing. The jewels are worth about £15,000. '
     'The safe had not been broken; it seems to have been opened by an expert who knew locks. The wire of the burglar alarm in the hall had been cut.\n'
     'I understand that a man named Horace Danby has been arrested and that his fingerprints are all over the room. He claims that I asked him to open the safe. This is nonsense – I was in London and have never met him. '
     'However, as he says a young lady in red was present, I request you to investigate whether a second person was involved.\n'
     'Kindly take action and help me recover my jewels.\n\nYours faithfully,\n(Owner’s wife), Shotover Grange.', lid='sr4')
